# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("What goes under artificial turf on Florida soil",
     "<p>Florida's new turf rule treats the base as part of the system, not an afterthought. DEP Rule 62-308.100 requires the subgrade under residential turf to be washed crushed rock or crushed concrete, never limerock or road base with fines, because fines bind together and seal the soil the way a driveway base is supposed to. Most lots around {city} sit on sandy flatwoods soils with a seasonal high water table that can sit within two feet of the surface for part of the year. A compacted aggregate lift that still drains is the only way to keep the lawn from holding water under the turf after a summer storm rolls through.</p>"),
    ("Does a sandy ridge lot near {city} still need a compacted base",
     "<p>Not every yard needs the same treatment. On the excessively drained ridge sands west of Orlando, water already moves through the sand profile in minutes, and some homeowners assume bare native sand is base enough. It isn't. DEP Rule 62-308.100 still calls for a defined subgrade of washed crushed rock or crushed concrete, compacted enough to carry foot traffic without being packed so tight that it blocks water from moving through, which the same rule prohibits. Around {city}, that usually means a thinner lift than a flatwoods lot needs, built from the same washed material rather than whatever fill happens to be on site.</p>"),
    ("Why a side-yard turf strip needs its own drainage path",
     "<p>A narrow run of turf between the house and the fence collects more water than a wide-open lawn panel does, because roof runoff, AC condensate and the neighbor's grade all drain toward the same few feet. The base in a side yard at a {city} home has to be built with a slight fall away from the foundation, not flat, so water keeps moving instead of sitting under the turf backing. Washed crushed rock is compacted in the same thin lifts used anywhere else on the property, but the finished grade gets checked with a level across the whole run before turf goes down, since there's no second chance to fix a low spot once it's covered.</p>"),
    ("Grading the base around a pool deck so turf drains away from the coping",
     "<p>Turf set against a pool deck has one job the base has to support: moving splash-out and rain away from the coping instead of toward it. The compacted aggregate base around a {city} pool gets graded with a gentle slope off the deck's edge, and the subgrade under it follows the same washed-rock, no-fines rule as every other part of the yard. Pool equipment pads, skimmer lines and deck drains all have to stay clear and functional once the turf is in, which means the base is built around them rather than over them, with the final grade checked at the coping before the turf is rolled out.</p>"),
    ("Why a backyard putting green needs a flatter, finer base than a lawn panel",
     "<p>A lawn panel can tolerate a little waviness; a putting surface can't. The aggregate base under a backyard green in {city} gets screeded and compacted in thinner lifts than a standard lawn area, so the finished plane reads true across slopes and breaks instead of showing every ripple in the subgrade. DEP Rule 62-308.100 still governs the material: washed crushed rock or crushed concrete, not limerock with fines, and the state rule caps how tight the subgrade can be compacted so water still percolates through it. A green that's compacted hard enough to putt true but still drains is a balance the base work has to get right the first time.</p>"),
    ("What a dog run's base has to do that a lawn's doesn't",
     "<p>A pet area turf base starts from the same rule as the rest of the yard, washed crushed rock or crushed concrete, never limerock or road base with fines, but it carries more liquid in one place over and over. Fines-heavy base material binds and seals, which is exactly what traps urine at the surface instead of letting it drain through; a properly washed, open-graded base keeps that liquid moving down and away. On a {city} lot, the run is also graded so rinse water drains off the pad rather than pooling at a low corner, since a dog run gets hosed down far more often than the rest of the lawn.</p>"),
    ("Setting turf into a paver pattern without changing the base rules",
     "<p>Turf inset as an accent strip between paver bands, along a walkway edge or inside a paver driveway, still has to sit on the same washed, crushed-rock subgrade the rule requires, not on leftover bedding sand from the paver install. Swapping sand for an open-graded aggregate under just the turf panel keeps that strip draining the way the rule intends, even though it's only a few inches wide next to pavers on a thicker compacted base. On a {city} property, the transition between the two materials is where water problems usually start, so the edge gets built up carefully rather than just buried under whichever base happened to already be there.</p>"),
    ("Working inside a live oak's drip line without hurting the roots",
     "<p>DEP Rule 62-308.100 bars turf inside a tree's drip line unless a certified arborist signs off that the install won't harm the roots, and live oaks around older {city} neighborhoods often have drip lines wide enough to cover most of a side yard. Where an arborist does approve the work, the base goes in by hand rather than with a plate compactor, in thin lifts, so the washed crushed rock fills around roots instead of crushing them. Machine compaction stays outside the root zone entirely. Several Central Florida cities also protect large live oaks by ordinance, so the arborist sign-off and the base method both matter before turf goes down near one.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("What the turf itself is made of, under Florida's new rule",
     "<p>Residential turf sold and installed in Florida has to clear DEP Rule 62-308.100's material standard: the turf, its backing and its infill can't contain heavy metals or intentionally added PFAS, and all of it has to be disposable at a landfill. Infill is limited to clean silica sand, rock, shell or other natural material, or silica sand with a non-toxic coating; loose rubber crumb is allowed only inside the footprint of playground equipment, not across a {city} lawn. Pile height and backing weight vary by product line, but the infill rule doesn't, it's the same whether the turf goes in a front yard or a side yard.</p>"),
    ("Why a dog run's infill has to stay natural too",
     "<p>A pet area doesn't get an exception to Florida's infill rule just because the use is heavier. The turf still has to use clean silica sand, rock, shell or coated silica sand as infill, the same list that applies to a front lawn, with rubber reserved only for playground equipment, not a dog run. Backing on pet-area turf is usually a more open, perforated design so urine and rinse water pass straight through to the base instead of sitting on top of the infill. On a {city} property, that open backing and natural infill work together: the material has to let liquid through, not just look like grass.</p>"),
    ("How a backyard putting green is built differently from the surrounding lawn",
     "<p>A putting surface uses a shorter, denser nylon or polyethylene face than a lawn-style turf panel, run with little or no infill so the ball rolls true instead of catching on loose sand or rock. Where a fringe of taller turf surrounds the green, that section is built, seamed and sometimes infilled more like a standard lawn, so the two sections meet at a visible but clean line. Florida's rule allows green synthetic turf without restriction on color, which covers both the putting surface and the fringe around it. On a {city} green, the cup and flagstick positions get set after the base is finished, not before, so the surface isn't disturbed.</p>"),
    ("Fitting turf panels into a paver pattern as an accent, not a patch",
     "<p>Turf set as a band inside a paver driveway or alongside a walkway gets its own edge restraint where it meets the pavers, tying into the paver's existing restraint rather than relying on the turf's perimeter nails alone. The panel is cut narrower than a full lawn roll and seamed along one or both long edges instead of four, which keeps the seam count down on a short run. Around {city}, this kind of inset strip usually runs the width of a driveway or along one side of a front walk, with the turf sitting slightly proud of the paver surface so mowing or trimming never touches it.</p>"),
    ("Choosing and setting turf around a pool deck",
     "<p>Turf around a pool gets fastened tight to the deck's edge restraint or coping bead, since that joint takes more foot traffic and more splash than any other seam in the yard. Chlorinated water and sunscreen residue don't break down most turf backings, but a seam placed right at the coping wears faster than a seam set back from the water, so the layout is planned to keep joints away from the busiest edge wherever it's practical. Infill still has to meet the state's natural-material rule even in a pool surround. On a {city} deck, drain lines and skimmer equipment get built around, with turf cut to the equipment rather than equipment moved to fit the turf.</p>"),
    ("Laying out turf in a narrow side yard to cut down on seams",
     "<p>A side yard that's only a few feet wide doesn't leave much room to hide a seam, so the roll is oriented to run the full length of the space whenever the width allows it, rather than being pieced together in short sections. Edge restraint runs along both the house and the fence line, since a narrow strip has two fixed edges instead of one open perimeter. On a {city} lot, gate hardware, hose bibs and utility meters in a side yard all get cut around individually, and each cut edge gets the same fastening as the outer perimeter so nothing works loose where foot traffic is heaviest.</p>"),
    ("Why in-ground sprinkler heads can't stay live under turf",
     "<p>DEP Rule 62-308.100 says in-ground irrigation can't be used to water a synthetic turf area, and a local government may require existing heads in that zone to be removed and the pipe capped below grade. Leaving a head live under turf doesn't just risk a rule violation, a buried, pressurized head eventually finds a seam or a low spot and pushes water up through it. The build spec calls for capping the lateral line and plugging the head location before the base goes in, not patching around a head that's still connected. On a {city} job, that irrigation zone is then either abandoned or redirected to beds and sod that still need it.</p>"),
    ("How seams and edges are anchored against wind and flooding",
     "<p>Florida's turf rule requires edges and seams to be anchored against wind and flooding, on top of whatever adhesive or seam tape the manufacturer specifies for the product itself. That usually means nail or spike spacing tightens up at the perimeter and along every seam, not just at the corners, so a gust under a loose edge can't start a peel that runs the length of a panel. Around a {city} yard, perimeter nailing also has to work around a septic tank's cleanout and lid, which the rule requires stay accessible for pump-outs rather than being nailed over or buried under turf.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("How a standard turf install runs from start to finish",
     "<p>The old lawn or landscaping comes out first, down to bare soil, followed by grading and compaction of the washed aggregate base in thin lifts rather than one thick pour. Turf rolls go down once the base passes a level check, get trimmed to the perimeter, and are seamed where two rolls meet before infill goes in and gets worked into the pile with a power broom. On a typical {city} yard, the base work and grading usually take longer than placing the turf itself, since a flat, compacted, draining subgrade is what the rest of the job depends on. Perimeter nailing and a final brushing finish the job.</p>"),
    ("Scheduling a turf install around Central Florida's rainy season",
     "<p>Base compaction needs dry, workable soil, which is harder to find in {city} between late May and mid-October, when afternoon storms are a near-daily pattern across both Central Florida and the Suncoast. A base compacted while saturated won't reach the density the job needs, and it can seal into a dense, impermeable layer that then fights the rule's percolation requirement instead of meeting it. Scheduling the grading and base lifts for a morning window, ahead of the typical afternoon buildup, keeps the material workable. Turf placement and seaming are far less weather-sensitive than the base work underneath them, so a stretch of wet afternoons affects the timeline more than it affects the finish.</p>"),
    ("The extra steps a backyard putting green adds to the schedule",
     "<p>A putting green needs more passes at the base stage than a lawn panel does, since the subgrade gets screeded and checked for a true plane rather than just a consistent compacted depth. Contours, breaks or tiers built into the green are shaped at this stage, before any turf goes down, because they can't be adjusted once the surface is seamed. Cup locations and the fringe transition are marked and cut after the main green panel is placed on a {city} green, not before, so the hole positions land where the finished surface actually breaks. The fringe area around the cups is seamed and infilled last.</p>"),
    ("What changes in the process for a pet area install",
     "<p>A dog run or pet area gets the same removal-grading-base sequence as the rest of a {city} yard, with one extra step: the perforated or open-weave backing and drainage layer get checked for flow before infill goes in, usually by running water across the pad and watching how fast it clears. Infill is worked into the pile more conservatively than on a show lawn, since a pet area holds more liquid over its life and a lighter infill layer drains and dries faster between uses. The perimeter gets nailed down tight along any edge where digging is likely.</p>"),
    ("Fitting a turf accent into an existing paver layout",
     "<p>Where turf goes in alongside pavers already set in {city}, the process starts with removing a strip of pavers and bedding sand rather than grading an open yard. The exposed subgrade is excavated a little deeper to fit the washed aggregate base for the turf, then compacted against the paver's existing edge restraint so the two systems tie together instead of shifting independently. The turf panel is cut to the exact width of the removed strip and seamed along its paver-side edge before the field edge is finished. Removed pavers are either reused elsewhere on the property or set aside for the homeowner.</p>"),
    ("Working turf installation around a functioning pool",
     "<p>A pool deck install has to protect the coping and the water itself through every stage, so base material and turf offcuts get kept off the pool surface and out of the skimmer, and equipment pads stay covered while grading happens nearby. On a {city} pool deck, the final seam at the coping is usually placed last, once the rest of the panel is set and trimmed, so the most exposed joint in the whole job gets the most attention and the least foot traffic during cure. Pool chemicals and standing water near the work area also push drying and seaming steps later in the day than they'd run elsewhere in the yard.</p>"),
    ("Why a narrow side yard takes longer than its square footage suggests",
     "<p>Material and equipment access through a {city} side yard is usually limited to a gate or a path a wheelbarrow can clear, which rules out larger compaction equipment and slows every stage down to what can be moved by hand. Base lifts go in with smaller, hand-guided plate compactors instead of ride-on equipment, and turf rolls that would normally be carried by two people get walked in sideways through a narrow gate. None of that changes what the finished base or turf has to meet, but a side yard with the same square footage as an open lawn panel routinely takes longer to finish.</p>"),
    ("Why an arborist visit can come before the install crew does",
     "<p>If turf is planned inside a live oak's drip line, the arborist certification required by DEP Rule 62-308.100 has to happen before any grading starts, not as a step folded into the install. Once that certification is in hand, the crew works the root zone by hand, digging and placing base material in small sections instead of trenching or running equipment across roots. On a {city} property with a mature oak close to the planned turf area, this adds a scheduling step up front, but it keeps the install from running into a stop-work problem halfway through a yard that's already been opened up.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("What happens when the base under turf isn't washed crushed rock",
     "<p>Limerock or road base with fines is cheaper and more common than washed crushed rock, which is exactly why it shows up under turf installs that skip the step DEP Rule 62-308.100 requires. Fines bind together once compacted and wet, sealing the subgrade instead of letting water pass through it, so a lawn that looked fine on a dry day in {city} starts holding standing water under the turf after the first real storm. The turf itself doesn't fail in that scenario, the base does, and by the time it shows up as a soft or soggy spot, the turf has to come up to fix what's underneath it.</p>"),
    ("Why compacting the base too hard can be its own mistake",
     "<p>The instinct on a sandy {city} lot is to compact the base as hard as it'll go, since a firm surface feels like a better foundation for turf. Florida's turf rule caps that instinct: the subgrade can't be compacted tight enough to stop water percolating through it, and a local government may set a specific maximum permeability standard the installed system has to meet. A base compacted past that point behaves almost like the fines problem, water that should move through the soil instead collects at the surface, under the turf, where nobody sees it until the yard starts smelling or feeling spongy underfoot.</p>"),
    ("The 10-foot rule for turf near a lake, pond or canal",
     "<p>Turf has to stay at least 10 feet from a natural or man-made waterbody under DEP Rule 62-308.100, measured from the ordinary or mean high water line, unless a seawall or bulkhead already separates the yard from the water. On a canal-front or lakefront {city} lot, that setback can cut a meaningful strip off a planned lawn panel, and it's easy to miss if the layout is measured from the yard's edge rather than from the water itself. The seawall exception doesn't apply just because the water is close; there has to be an actual physical barrier in place, not just a slope down to the waterline.</p>"),
    ("Installing turf under a live oak without checking the drip line first",
     "<p>A live oak's drip line can extend well past where the canopy visually seems to end, and DEP Rule 62-308.100 bars turf inside that line unless a certified arborist certifies the install won't harm the roots. Skipping that step on a {city} property with a mature oak risks root damage during grading and base compaction, plus a rule violation that has nothing to do with how good the finished turf looks. Several Central Florida cities also protect large live oaks by ordinance, independent of the state turf rule, so a drip-line mistake near one of those trees can trigger a second problem on top of the first.</p>"),
    ("The problem with leaving an old sprinkler head under new turf",
     "<p>DEP Rule 62-308.100 bars using in-ground irrigation to water a synthetic turf area, which means every head that used to cover that zone has to be dealt with, not just switched off at the controller. A head left live and buried under turf in {city} eventually pressurizes against the turf backing, finds a low spot or a seam, and pushes water up through it on a schedule nobody controls. What looks like a drainage problem months later is often an irrigation problem that was never actually fixed, just hidden under a new surface that made it harder to find.</p>"),
    ("Why 'the HOA can't stop me' isn't quite true for turf",
     "<p>Florida's statute preempting local governments from restricting compliant turf, F.S. 125.572, regulates city and county rules, not HOA architectural review, an association in {city} can still apply its own approval process to a turf project. Separately, F.S. 720.3045 only protects turf that isn't visible from the parcel's frontage, an adjacent parcel or a community golf course; a front yard facing the street doesn't qualify just because the material itself is allowed under state law. Checking the HOA's documents before digging saves a redo that the DEP rule alone won't prevent.</p>"),
    ("Why turf gets hot enough to matter in full Florida sun",
     "<p>Dark-colored turf fiber and infill both heat up fast in direct sun, and a panel installed with no shade at all across a {city} yard will get noticeably hotter underfoot than the surrounding pavers or lawn by mid-afternoon. Bare feet, paws and pets lying down on it are the real complaint, not the turf material itself failing. A quick hose-down before use cools the surface for a while without running afoul of the rule against in-ground irrigation on turf, since hand-watering with a hose isn't the same system the rule restricts. Shade structures and tree cover, where the drip-line rule allows them, help more than any turf product choice does.</p>"),
    ("What a hurricane season does to turf that wasn't anchored right",
     "<p>Wind-driven rain gets under a loose turf edge faster than most homeowners expect, and once it does, the same gust that lifted the edge can peel a seam open across a {city} yard if the nailing or seam tape underneath wasn't set to the rule's standard for anchoring against wind and flooding. Oak leaves, pine needles and other storm debris then work their way into the open seam, which both looks bad and blocks the drainage the base below is supposed to provide. Checking every seam and the full perimeter after a named storm catches a lifted edge before the next one makes it worse.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Keeping leaves and storm debris from matting the turf",
     "<p>Oak leaves, pine needles and seed pods are a bigger issue for turf than for a natural lawn, because they don't break down into the surface the way they would in soil, they sit on top of the pile and, left long enough, mat it down and block drainage through the infill. A leaf blower or a stiff broom on a regular pass keeps a {city} yard's turf clear without disturbing the infill the way a hard rake can. After a windy stretch or a storm, a full pass matters more than usual, since that's when the most debris ends up pressed into the pile at once.</p>"),
    ("Why infill needs occasional top-ups, not just brushing",
     "<p>Infill settles and migrates over years of foot traffic, rain and brushing, and a turf panel that looked fully filled at installation can thin out at high-traffic paths or low spots in a {city} yard well before the turf fibers themselves show wear. A top-up uses the same natural material the rule requires at installation, clean silica sand, rock, shell or coated silica sand, not whatever happens to be on hand, since the material standard doesn't relax for a maintenance pass. Brushing the pile upright after a top-up redistributes the new infill evenly instead of leaving it in a pile at the low point.</p>"),
    ("Rinsing a pet area without relying on the sprinkler system",
     "<p>Because DEP Rule 62-308.100 bars using in-ground irrigation on turf, a dog run's rinse routine has to run on a hose instead of a zone on the controller, which is actually closer to how the open, perforated backing in a pet area is designed to work. A quick rinse after use moves waste and odor through the backing and into the base below rather than letting it sit on the infill surface. On a {city} property, keeping a hose bib within reach of the pet area makes that rinse routine realistic to keep up with, rather than something that only happens occasionally.</p>"),
    ("Checking seams and edges after storm season, not just once a year",
     "<p>A seam or edge that was anchored correctly at installation can still work loose over a season of heat cycling, wind and the occasional named storm moving through {city}, so a walk around the full perimeter and every seam after a significant storm catches a lifted spot before it grows. Re-securing a loose edge early is a small fix; leaving it through another storm turns it into a bigger one, since each gust that gets under an open edge peels it back a little further. This check costs nothing but time and doesn't require pulling up any turf to do it.</p>"),
    ("Keeping turf clear of the drainage paths it sits next to",
     "<p>DEP Rule 62-308.100 bars turf from a swale, ditch, stormwater pond or a pond's littoral zone, and the same logic applies to ongoing care: infill, trimmed edges and loose debris from a {city} yard shouldn't wash into those drainage features even when the turf itself is set back from them correctly. Keeping the turf's edge clean and the infill topped up where it borders a swale keeps material from migrating downhill during heavy rain. A drainage path that's been narrowed by stray infill or buildup over a season or two is worth clearing before it becomes a bigger problem for the whole street.</p>"),
    ("Why weeds show up at turf seams and edges first",
     "<p>Seeds blow in and settle at a seam or a perimeter edge before they settle in the middle of an open panel, since that's where wind-blown debris and a little extra soil tend to collect over time on a {city} lawn. Pulling a stray weed at a seam while it's small keeps its roots from working into the backing or lifting an edge as it grows. A board or a stiff brush dragged along the perimeter during a regular debris pass usually catches this before it needs a dedicated trip, since it's the same edges that get checked after storms anyway.</p>"),
    ("What a backyard putting green needs that a lawn panel doesn't",
     "<p>A putting surface in {city} gets a light sand topdressing on a schedule rather than infill top-ups the way a lawn panel does, since the goal on a green is a consistent, true roll rather than a filled, upright pile like the surrounding fringe grass. The fringe around the cups is trimmed back periodically so it doesn't creep onto the putting surface and change how a ball breaks near the edge. Cups and flagsticks get checked for wear and a secure fit in the cup liner, since that's hardware a lawn panel never has to maintain at all.</p>"),
    ("Keeping septic access clear under a turf lawn",
     "<p>DEP Rule 62-308.100 requires septic tank access to stay reachable even with turf installed over or near the drain field, which in practice means the access lid location on a {city} property is marked and either left turf-free or fitted with a removable panel rather than nailed down like the rest of the lawn. Pump-out crews need to reach that lid without cutting turf to find it, so marking the location clearly at installation saves a repair later. A turf panel nailed solid over a septic lid turns a routine pump-out into an unplanned turf repair.</p>"),
  ],
}
